				<td colspan="2"><span class="postbody">“Her highness is so very, very very..., quite um,  well I'd say um very stout." Boronor Runeaxe the tanner said with a wave of an ale in his short stubby fingers. "Lovely bride she'll make old Graybeard." 
<br />

<br />
Standing by the tannery while she waited for the finishing stitches to be put on the leather overcloak, Gwentha Cobblestone wished it was she marrying the dwarf from Velious instead of the princess. It sounded so exciting to be leaving the fort and traveling the world beyond Kaladim. Unfortunately traveling required courage and funds, two things she was very much in severe need. Listening to the tanner she pounced on the one piece of information that might be her key to adventure. "Old Graybeard has many young dwarves with him?" 
<br />

<br />
"Aye, he does. All be warriors firm and true, and dressed in metals, not the leather this old tanner makes." 
<br />

<br />
"But surely they'll be needing their under tunics washed." Gwentha said even as she felt the heat rising up her jaw. She could have kicked herself for mentioning men's underthings to the tanner. Her mother would have swatted her ears. 
<br />

<br />
Boronor gave her a rough glance and lifted his bushy brows in question. "I don't think this is something ye should be talking about lass. Mayhap you should go see ifn your mother needs help." 
<br />

<br />
Gwentha sighed. Of course her mother needed help. Her mother always needed help. As under seamstress to one of the Queen's maids, her mother's, and consequently her own tasks, seemed endless. She started to go then turned, "I'll be back on the morrow for the cloak." 
<br />

<br />
"Day after. Me orders are backed up fer the weddin." Boronor replied. 
<br />

<br />
Now it was Gwentha who lifted a brow. "I thought you said Old Graybeard's men didn't use leather." 
<br />

<br />
"He don't. But their women, now that's another matter." 
<br />

<br />
Gwentha resisted a strong urge to swear at Brell.  The day after tomorrow was too late.  She needed the cloak back by tomorrow night. How else would she make herself presentable for the royal wedding if she didn't have it.  This was almost too much.  She felt ready to cry.  She'd been pestering old Boronor for 3 weeks now to finish the garment and time was running out.   Her only green dress was too old and stained to wear to a royal wedding.  Plus she couldn't wear green to a dwarven wedding.  The folk would think she was trying to act like a druid.  She needed the brown cloak to cover the unsightly color.
<br />

<br />
Drawing a deep breath she was prepared to start begging when Dame Bulamyr, the lady her mother sewed for,  came bustling down the path.  “There you are, lassie, I've been looking everywhere.” she grabbed Gwentha's hand and practically pulled her away from the tanner.   “I just heard the most awful news.”
<br />

<br />
Fixing her face in a tight smile, Gwentha allowed herself to be drug along, all the while reminding herself that Dame Bulamyr provided her with food.   Never in her entire life had the Dame's bad news been anything serious.  If anything it would some sort of dire task that would have her scampering around trying to finish it right until the party began.  Which meant she wouldn't have time to get her cloak repaired.  
<br />

<br />
Steeling herself for the worst, Gwentha followed the dame past the town fountain and down a tunnel towards the lady's house.  Only when they entered the house's private garden did she notice the smells of roast aviak in the air.  
<br />

<br />
“And so I was saying, the news involves your mother....” The dame was saying.  
<br />

<br />
“Me mother?”
<br />

<br />
Dame Bulamyr yanked on Gwentha's wrist.  “Lassie are you listening?”
<br />

<br />
“Of course me lady,  what about me mother?”  Gwentha asked with genuine concern.  
<br />

<br />
The Dame studied her shrewdly from gristled eyes.   “Yer mother has informed me that we've no batwing fur for me parasol.”
<br />

<br />
“Oh.” Gwentha said feeling relief.
<br />

<br />
“I can't go to the dock sendoff to Velious after the weddin without one.  Everyone there will have one and it taint right for me not to be sprouting one too.”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha couldn't believe she'd just been drug all the way across town for a decorative umbrella.  “Well mayhap we could use the eel skin from last century's  raincloak.  Such a lovely shade of gray ought to make you stylish.”
<br />

<br />
Dame Bulamyr thunked her over the head with her walking stick.  “Eel skin will never do.  Everyone would think me for an inky.   It must be batwing.  I must have it.  And you be the only lassie I can spare to find it.”
<br />

<br />
“I suppose I can go to town.”Gwentha said cautiously.   “But it might be costly.”
<br />

<br />
“Yee can't go to town.  I've already tried that.  There's no bat to be had.  You'll have to go to he merchants beyond the gate.” the dame said handing her a small bag of silver.
<br />

<br />
“The gate?  I can't do that.  I've never been outside the walls of Kaladim in all me life.”  
<br />

<br />
Dame Bulamyr harumphed.  “It's about time ye ventured then.  And be quick about it.  We've got to have time to stretch the fur so it fits the parasol.”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha tried to protest.  “I can't go out there.  Me mother says the outside is full of danger.”
<br />

<br />
“Then take me walking stick.  But hurry back.  I need it for getting around.”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha took the stick and held it uneasily.
<br />

<br />
“Don't hold it like that lass.  Hold it like this, so it's ready for thunking.”  Dame Bulamyr said as she readjusted Gwentha's grip.  “There now your ready for bashing.”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha couldn't believe this was happening.  Her entire life she'd imagined it, a chance to go up to the world beyond and now that it was happening she was scared to death.  “Thank ye, me lady.” she said with a slight curtsy. 
<br />

<br />
“Hurry on.  Time's wastin.” 
<br />

<br />
Not giving herself a chance to change her mind she took the coin and the stick and hurried through town up towards the elusive main gate.   When she reached the gate she stopped, took a deep breath and walked into the sun.
<br />

<br />
The sun was much brighter than she expected.  So bright that at first she couldn't see.  Behind her the gate guards were chuckling as she stumbled back into Kaladim's protective shadows.  
<br />

<br />
“Take it slowly, lass.  One eye at a time.”  One of the guards offered helpfully.
<br />

<br />
Nodding thanks, Gwentha rubbed her eyes and tried again.  She opened one eye slowly, taking in the colors piece by piece.   What she saw amazed her.  Butcherblock mountains lay before her basking in the morning sunshine.  It was immense.   Green grass spread out leading up to distant bluish foothills.   Beyond that earthy mountain tips touched a clear blue sky.   She was dimly aware of the sound of the lapping ocean, mixed with the screech of aviak cries.   The air was fresh with the smell of earth and dew.   Gwentha opened the other eye and  took a tentative step forward.  She turned her face upwards  and  enjoyed the feel of the sun on her features. 
<br />

<br />
There was a shout.  Gwentha stared in it's direction and saw a bare chested human running toward her.  Behind him stretched a trail of angry goblins.  It wasn't the goblins that horrified her, it was the human's hairless chest.  
<br />

<br />
“Out of the way, lass.” the guard said, flinging her to the side.   Gwentha's back smacked into stone of Kaladim's outer wall as the human lad ran past.   Drawing their weapons the guards   killed the goblins within feet of her, splattering their gore all over her only dress.  Gwentha got to her feet stunned.   She stepped gingerly away from the dead goblins and noticed the greenish blood on her hem.   
<br />

<br />
“Ohh ohhh.  Look what you've done?   I'll never get this stain out.”
<br />

<br />
The guard gave her a sideways glance.  “Why would ye want to lass.  That blood is a mark of courage.  Ye didn't run when 5 goblins attacked.  Ye should be proud ye stood yer ground.”
<br />

<br />
Stood her ground?  Gwentha hadn't exactly been standing when the goblins arrived.   She rubbed her bruised derriere where it had smacked into the keep's outer walls, and continued backing away.  “But this is my only dress.  Me mother will kill me.  Whatever will I wear to the wedding.”
<br />

<br />
“Awe lass, don't be worried too much about yer appearace. I  think you look fine meself.    But here ifn it makes yee feel better ye can have any tattered silk ye can pick off the goblins.”  He offered her a gory pair of boots.   
<br />

<br />
At first Gwentha was going to refuse.  But then she looked closer.  True the material was old, dirty and torn but it had once been fine and with a good washing and darning it could be made passable.  Besides she'd never worn cloth boots.  She went and inspected the rest of the goblins and found pants and gloves to match.   
<br />

<br />
“I can never thank you enough.” she told the guards after she was finished salvaging.  “But now can you tell me where I might find Bat fur?”
<br />

<br />
“Up there on the hill.” one of the pointed, towards the bluish foot hills.
<br />

<br />
Gwentha thought that was a strange place for a merchant but she'd already seen enough of the outside to know that things here were not as they were in Kaladim.  She headed up towards the hill, and only when she'd reached the top, did she realize that the bats here were a little too fresh for buying.   They were still alive and flying overhead.  Gwentha ducked for the ground when one dove at her, only relaxing slightly when she realized its' target was a fat mosquito and not the top of her head.  She turned back towards the gate intending to run back to the guards when a young male voice said, “Hey you come help me”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha turned towards the sound.  It was the human who had brought all the goblins to the guards.  He was trying to smack a snake.   “What are you doing?”  she asked.
<br />

<br />
“Killing this moss snake.”
<br />

<br />
“With your bare hands?  Are you daft?”
<br />

<br />
He gave her an annoyed look.  “The hands can be just as deadly as a sword.”
<br />

<br />
“Ifn you say so.  Why would you even want to kill it?”
<br />

<br />
“So I can sell it's skin for coppers.  The merchants over that way will pay money for it.”
<br />

<br />
“Merchants?  What merhants.”
<br />

<br />
“Over yonder, past this hill near the keep.”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha started walking that way when the human called again, “Hey aren't you going to help me?”
<br />

<br />
She stopped and looked at him.  He was sweating and looked tired.   “What do you want me to do?”
<br />

<br />
“Heal me if you can.”
<br />

<br />
“I can't heal you. I'm not a cleric. I'm a seamstress.”
<br />

<br />
“Then use your stick and hit it.”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha looked uneasy.  She could just imagine the emergency Dame Bulamyr would create if she returned the walking stick covered in snake guts.  No doubt she'd be  sent back outside spending more days out here in the sun looking for a replacement staff and Gwentha had already had enough of this world outside to last her a hundred years.
<br />

<br />
“Help me.” the boy shouted between pants.
<br />

<br />
Turning her head, she saw his distress and hesitated.  “I can't.  I'm afraid of snakes.”
<br />

<br />
“If you don't I'll die.” he answered.
<br />

<br />
Gwentha watched him try and smack the slithery reptile and her conscious got the better of her.  “What are ye doing swinging yer fists.  Have ye no sense in yer head?”
<br />

<br />
“What I'm doing isn't easy, it takes a lot of practice.”
<br />

<br />
“I think your daffy.   Even a girl like me knows ye don't attack a wild animal with your hands..  Ye has to be far enough away so it can't bite you.”  she said as he trotted back over to him.  Lifting the stick she took a swing with Dame Bulamyr's staff.  To her horror she missed.  
<br />

<br />
The human laughed so she took another swing and clubbed it.  It coiled back stunned then started slithering away.  She and the lad  both chased it and finished it.
<br />

<br />
Once it was dead the boy scooped up the flattened carcass and stuck it in a bag on his waist.   He turned away and began eyeing a wandering Aviak chick.   
<br />

<br />
“Yer welcome.” Gwentha said as she watched him creep up on the birdling.
<br />

<br />
“Oh thanks for the help.” He turned back towards here.  “Do you want to come kill this?”
<br />

<br />
Gwentha shook her head.  “I can't.  I have to go buy bat wings.”
<br />

<br />
He lifted a brow.  “Why don't you just kill bats.”
<br />

<br />
“Do what?”  Gwentha looked outraged.  “I can't kill them.  I wouldn't know what do with them once they were dead.”
<br />

<br />
“Just use your fingers to rip their wings off.  It's easy.” the boy said as he turned his attention away from the chick and ran after a nearby bat.  He smacked at it the flying creature causing it to dive down and  try and bite him.
<br />

<br />
Gwentha watched in disbelief as he managed to kill it with only his fingers.  It hadn't looked that hard, but still she preferred using the staff.   She clutched it protectively while ripped off the wings and tossed them at her.  “There's more bats over that hill, he said pointing off in the distance.  Come with me and we'll get more of them.”
<br />

<br />
She knew she shouldn't.  She knew this was a bad idea.  She knew she shouldn't run off with a stranger, and a human at that but the idea sounded too exciting.  Besides she'd never forgive herself if she didn't go and the boy got himself killed.  So she followed.
<br />

<br />
Later that day she returned to Dame Bulamyr's house with an entire stack of wings.  She was feeling proud of her accomplishment but the Dame had another reaction.   
<br />

<br />
“Girl what have ye done to me walking stick.  It's covered in guts?”
<br />

<br />
“I killed the bats instead of buying them.” Gwentha said offering the dame her bag of silver.
<br />

<br />
“Ye went bat killing?  Ye covered my stick in guts?  Ye've lost yer senses.  Yer lucky you weren't killed yourself.” the dame wrung her hands.  “Now what weapon will I carry to the weddin?”
<br />

<br />
“How bout a mace or a flail.  I could buy one in the market.”  Gwentha offered.
<br />

<br />
“Too expensive.”  The dame's shrewd eyes focused on her.  “I need the staff fer walking. It helps with the corns on me toes.  Nay lassie I think you'll just have to find me a replacement.  Ye have all night and half a day tomorrow.  Ye better get to it.”
<br />

<br />
So Gwentha found herself back outside in Butcherblock mountains.  The sky was a pearlescent pink with the setting sun and a cool salty breeze wafted in from the ocean. She wished she had stopped by Boronor's tanner for her torn overcloak.  It would provide some protection from the coming night's chill.   Knowing the tannery was already closed,  she looked off in the direction of bat hill and wondered if the human lad was still around.  Maybe he knew where she could find another staff for Dame Bulamyr...
<br />

